A folder Windows marked read-only is still a folder its owner can delete.

ntfs3 builds a directory's mode from fmask and dmask and then clears every
write bit when the entry carries FILE_ATTRIBUTE_READONLY. The masks are applied
first, so no value of fmask, dmask or umask survives it, and this version
parses no option that governs the mapping.

What reaches Finder is dr-xr-xr-x. Finder reads the mode, decides the delete
cannot work and says the item is in use, which is neither true nor anything
anybody can act on: the directory takes new entries perfectly well underneath.
Three folders on a 247 GB drive were undeletable that way.

The attribute is still read, still kept, and still written back by a chmod that
drops the write bits. It no longer decides what the owner of the mount may do.

diff -ruN a/fs/ntfs3/inode.c b/fs/ntfs3/inode.c
--- a/fs/ntfs3/inode.c	2025-12-12 00:00:00
+++ b/fs/ntfs3/inode.c	2026-09-12 00:00:00
@@ -1,4 +1,5 @@
 // SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0
+// Modified on 2026-09-12 by Clement Rahula <lukotta@rahula.dev>: the read-only attribute does not clear the mode's write bits.
 /*
